Phase **1a** of the plan in #1466 (see [the plan comment](https://github.com/noonghunna/club-3090/issues/1466#issuecomment-5865539004)). This is the foundation the rest of #1466, #1467 and #1465 builds on. **Nothing calls it yet, so no behaviour changes.** Phase 1b moves the readers onto it.

## What's in it

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `scripts/lib/club_config.py` | The Python loader, and the **one writer**. |
| `scripts/lib/club-config.sh` | The bash twin: `club_config_resolve`, `club_config_load`, `club_config_set`, `club_config_unset`. |
| `scripts/tests/test-club-config.sh` | Parity, rules, writer and redaction tests. |
| `scripts/tests/test-config-single-parser.sh` | A new **Tier 0** guard; it is also added to the Tier 0 list in `AGENTS.md`. |

**Store.** `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/club-3090/`, overridable with `CLUB3090_CONFIG_DIR`:
- `club3090.env` holds settings;
- `secrets.env` (mode 0600) holds tokens and keys;
- the repo `.env` is still read, last.

**Precedence:** shell > `club3090.env` > `secrets.env` > repo `.env`. A value set in the shell wins even when it is empty.

**One set of parsing rules.** The old readers disagreed on these, for example `switch.sh` let the first duplicate win and `repo_dotenv.py` the last:
- within one file, the last assignment of a key wins;
- one pair of surrounding quotes is stripped;
- nothing is expanded;
- CRLF line endings and an `export ` prefix are tolerated;
- keys that aren't shell identifiers are skipped.

**The writer:**
- keeps comments and key order;
- replaces the file atomically, under a lock;
- creates `secrets.env` as 0600 and the directory as 0700;
- refuses values that bash, `docker compose --env-file` and systemd would not all read the same way (quotes, `$`, backslashes, `#`-comments, surrounding whitespace, newlines).

`club_config.py resolve` hides secret values unless given `--show-secrets`. That covers anything read from `secrets.env`, and any credential-looking name (`*TOKEN`, `*_KEY`, …) wherever it came from.

## Tests

`test-club-config`:
- **bash and Python produce byte-identical output**, and both match a **golden list**, so the two can't be wrong the same way. A self-test confirms the parity check can fail.
- precedence, including an empty value set in the shell;
- `load`, and the config-directory rules;
- file modes; rewrites that keep comments; 12 refusals;
- a **round trip through real `docker compose --env-file`**, which is the path values take into containers;
- 20 concurrent writers lose nothing;
- redaction.

Mutation checks: swapping the precedence order, a first-assignment-wins parser, and removing redaction each turn it red.

`test-config-single-parser` is a **ratchet**:
- The 15 files that still read `.env` their own way are listed, each with the phase that moves it.
- A new reader fails, and so does a listed file that no longer reads `.env`, so the list can only shrink.
- Its self-test covers 9 reader shapes and 7 look-alikes (`local.env`, `--env-file /dev/null`, comments, docstrings).
- Both arms were mutation-checked on the real tree.

It also caught a bug in its own first draft: under `pipefail`, `grep | grep -q` SIGPIPEs on large files, so real readers read as clean. The self-test now includes a 20,000-line fixture for that.

Tier 0, `test-locale-utf8`, `test-script-permissions`, `test-docs-local-layer` and `test-registry-emit-no-yaml` all pass.

## Notes for review

- One thing to check in 1b: `switch.sh` and `launch.sh` currently look at any *set* shell variable, exported or not (`${!k+x}`), when deciding "shell wins". The bash loader keeps that rule; the Python one looks at `os.environ`. That's identical for launcher use, and it's pinned by the parity test.
- `switch.sh`'s loader comment cites `#425` for the shell-wins rule; #425 is an unrelated slug PR. The new code doesn't repeat it, and the old comment goes away in 1b.

