## Bug

`switch.sh` brought the running slug down **first** and only then ran the checks that can refuse a launch. So a refused launch left the rig serving nothing. We hit it twice on the reference rig in one day:
- an experimental slug run without `--force`;
- a worktree whose model files weren't on the host (no `MODEL_DIR`).

A mistyped slug did the same.

## Fix

A new `check_variant` runs, before `down_running`, every refusal that doesn't need the old slug's resources back:
- unknown slug;
- the `--force` status gate;
- missing compose file;
- `preflight_compose_deps` (model files);
- `preflight_compose_hardware` (GPU count, SM and *total* VRAM);
- `preflight_offload_split_mode` (reads only `SPLIT_MODE`);
- a dry run of the engine-pin resolver, in a subshell so its exports still land where they always have.

The checks that measure **free** VRAM or host RAM stay after the teardown, which is what frees them: `gpu_fit`, `lmcache_ram`, `cpu_offload_ram`, the offload resolvers and `gpu_preflight`.

`launch.sh` delegates to `switch.sh`, so it's covered too.

## Test

`test-switch-refuse-before-teardown` drives the **real** `switch.sh` against a `docker` shim. The shim reports one running club container and logs every teardown and `compose up`.

| Case | master | this PR |
|---|---|---|
| experimental slug, no `--force` | refused **after** tearing the running slug down | refused, running slug left up |
| unknown slug | same | refused, left up |
| model files missing (`MODEL_DIR` → empty dir) | same | refused, left up |
| positive control: a launch that passes | torn down, then up | torn down, then up |

Also green:
- `test-tests-never-launch` (the new test's calls are shimmed);
- `test-switch-orphan-teardown`, `test-thinking-pin`, `test-switch-registry-parity`, `test-launch-registry-parity`, `test-launch-compat`, `test-preflight-compose-deps`;
- Tier 0 guards;
- the rest of the tests that invoke `switch.sh`.

